	<h1 class="page-title">Consulting &amp; assignments</h1>
	<p class="page-lede">Energy policy, power sector governance and international cooperation — in France and internationally.</p>
	<div class="page-body">

<p>I work with institutions, operators, local governments and consultancies on energy policy, power sector governance and international cooperation. Short and medium-length assignments, in France and internationally, with one simple principle: the work starts from the client's files, not from a catalogue.</p>

<h2>Types of assignments</h2>

<p><strong>Sector diagnostics and framing notes.</strong> Electricity markets and grids, regulation, concessions, sector reforms. Institutional interviews, documentary and regulatory analysis, international comparisons, organisational recommendations.</p>

<p><strong>Capacity building and training design.</strong> Catalogues, career-track curricula and modules for executives, elected officials and staff, from initial diagnosis to delivery. Recent example: a full training catalogue designed for a Cameroonian public agency (see below).</p>

<p><strong>Stakeholder engagement and social acceptability.</strong> Dialogue with users and local authorities, tariff pedagogy, stakeholder mapping. Acceptability is treated as a risk factor on reform outcomes, not as a communications issue.</p>

<p><strong>Institutional and crisis communications.</strong> Sensitive files, environmental controversies, relations with elected officials, administrations and the media.</p>

<h2>Case study: Cameroon (2026)</h2>

<p>Independent field mission in Yaoundé, July 2026, within the environment of the World Bank-financed Power Sector Reform Programme (PRSEC, a <em>Program-for-Results</em> operation).</p>

<ul>
<li>Institutional interviews across the sector: PRSEC coordination unit (Ministry of Water and Energy), Rural Electrification Agency&nbsp;/&nbsp;PERACE, AFD Yaoundé (Infrastructure division).</li>
<li>Deliverables handed to the programme coordinator on 22 July 2026: a vision note proposing to develop AREYA into a regional school for Central Africa's electricity workforce; a 2026-2027 training catalogue (23 modules, 6 career tracks, 1 regional capstone project); a technical support proposal.</li>
<li>Documents designed to serve the results indicators of a <em>Program-for-Results</em> operation (disbursement tied to results, not expenditure) and the regional integration agenda of the Central African Power Pool (CAPP/PEAC).</li>
<li>A short module on securing reform outcomes: user acceptability, tariff pedagogy after a prolonged freeze, smart meter roll-out, engagement with users and local authorities.</li>
<li>Invited lecture at the Cameroon Institute of International Relations (IRIC, University of Yaoundé II), 29 July 2026: "The geopolitics of electricity in Central Africa: sovereignty, regulation and interconnections".</li>
<li>Published analysis following the mission: <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/cameroon-has-the-power-it-cannot-deliver-it/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">"Cameroon Has the Power. It Cannot Deliver It."</a>, African Arguments (Royal African Society), 2 August 2026.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Methods</h2>

<p>Semi-structured institutional interviews, documentary and regulatory analysis, comparative case studies, multi-criteria international benchmarking, stakeholder mapping, training design. Written and oral deliverables in French and English.</p>

<h2>Working framework</h2>

<ul>
<li>Expert registered with the <strong>African Development Bank (AfDB)</strong> since 2025.</li>
<li>Geographic scope: France and Europe, francophone Africa, Indo-Pacific. Immediately available for international travel, short and medium assignments.</li>
<li>Working languages: French, English.</li>
</ul>

<p>Assignments are carried out in an independent capacity. My publications, op-eds and teaching engage only myself.</p>

